{"id":"FLOW-E06","prompt":"A student draws a loop that checks N <= 100 before adding N, starts with N=1 and Sum=0, adds N, increments N, and returns to the test. Explain whether this is pre-test or post-test, whether the body could run zero times if the initial N were changed to 101, and why.","responseType":"concept explanation with trace condition","marks":5,"expectedAnswer":"It is pre-test because the condition is checked before the body. If N starts at 101, 101<=100 is false immediately, so the body runs zero times and the chart proceeds to output. The condition controls entry into the loop.","rubric":[{"criterion":"Identifies the loop as pre-test","points":1},{"criterion":"Explains the condition is checked before loop work","points":1},{"criterion":"Evaluates 101<=100 as false","points":1},{"criterion":"States the body runs zero times","points":1},{"criterion":"Connects the result to pre-test behavior and chart exit","points":1}],"source":"PDF p. 12, 15; flow.md ‘Pre-test and post-test loops’","operation":"infer behavior from loop test position"},
